# First Requests cold development cohort

All eight scheduled events reached terminal outcomes. Each arm accepted 5414 and
failed to accept 6028, so no two-node trajectory completed. Candidate grading
success and accepted-for-next-state success both equal 1/2 nodes for every arm.

| Arm | Provider requests | Provider total tokens | Cached input tokens | Controller wall (s) | Outer invocation wall (s) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| A | 1 | 14,428 | 13,568 | 96.424 | 96.745 |
| B | 4 | 65,695 | 15,104 | 408.510 | unavailable |
| C | 1 | 14,589 | 512 | 285.488 | 285.790 |
| partial D | 1 | 14,269 | 512 | 506.814 | 507.358 |

The total is 7 provider requests and 108,981 provider-reported tokens, including all
failed attempts. Every request had a durable intent and terminal usage receipt.
There are 16 unique native grader invocations: 14 direct calls and 2 native sealing
replays. All 16 completed; 6 passed and 10 failed. Repeated appearances of the same
replay receipt are counted once. Enclosing runtime includes context, symbolic,
world, grading and native publication/read work; their component timers are not
added again. Docker CPU measurements are unavailable.

A/C/D's accepted first edits produced an actual Git conflict in `models.py` when
carried onto the public 6028 base. The original retrieval selected optional lexical
modules before appending that complete conflict module: four modules totaling
80,552 raw bytes produced a 91,202-byte request, above the frozen 65,536-byte cap.
These three nodes stopped before provider dispatch and retained the conflict and
earlier accepted source. Their zero additional expenditure is a profile failure,
not a successfully avoided repair request.

B's first model proposed a different accepted edit, and the actual three-way
merge completed without conflict. Its 6028 current grade was 185/187 designated
checks; subsequent candidates scored 184/187, 185/187 and 185/187. All three attempts
and their 51,503 tokens remain in the fixed-cohort totals. The eight outside-
designated fixture errors are retained in the native evaluation scope. No native
timeout, OOM or environment drift caused these four failing B6028 grades.

The outer driver terminated with exit 143 while B5414's original worker remained
live. That worker completed normally at the controller/event level and was never
restarted. Reconciliation verified its committed terminal stdout and absence of
pending effects, then launched only the already scheduled B6028 event. B5414's
complete outer invocation timer, waited-child CPU and process exit code could not
be recovered and remain null; its controller timer is retained. The cause of the
driver signal was unobserved.

All first-node model requests were byte-identical across arms. B used exact raw
fallback after native context preparation, with zero qualified source omissions.
C/D's native symbolic candidates were actually graded and failed before model
repair. D additionally completed real native publication and a later verified
source read, each with a fresh ordinary grader replay. These observations do not
identify general or causal token savings: this is one adaptively developed family,
each arm has one trajectory, failures differ at the second node, and provider
prompt caching was uncontrolled. Lifecycle reuse, certified procedures and learned
world utility remain outside partial D's implementation.

The exact ledger/source/provider audit is `root_final_ledger_audit_v2.json`:
1,769 checks pass, model/effect accounting is complete, and total outer wall
accounting is explicitly incomplete. Root independently joined all 7 provider
receipts in `root_fixed_cohort_analysis_v1.json`. Curator scorer joins are in
`../../../recruitment/candidate_scoring_v1/requests_runtime_accounting_v1/final_admission_v2.json`
(SHA256 5d4dd8c12671f1e08b4b58063749f69bb08f1e0ffcf8bade80388b542ed69038).
Qualification and prior engineering costs are separate from this runtime cohort.
